    "measurementTechnique": [
      "In a tropical montane forest in Northern Borneo, airborne LiDAR data were collected in 2012 and 2017 at site 1 (2.67 km x 8 km) and site 2 (2 km x 8 km). With a scan angle of 22.5\u00b0 and a pulse repetition frequency of 240 kHz, the Riegl LMS-Q560 system placed on a helicopter that flew at the height of around 400 m above sea level was used to acquire the 2012 LiDAR data. The Optech's ALTM Orion C200 system installed on a fixed-wing aircraft (Nomad C22) was used to scan the 2017 LiDAR data at an altitude of approximately 600 m above sea level with a scan angle and pulse repetition frequency of 20\u00b0 and 240 kHz.",
      "The LiDAR data of 2012 and 2017 were processed to generate LiDAR-derived variables.",
      "Field data were collected using square plots in 2012 (76 plots) and 2017 (56 plots) at site 1 (2.67 km x 8 km) and site 2 (2 km x 8 km).",
      "Field AGB was calculated based on allometry of Chave et al. (2014).",
      "A stepwise multiple linear regression was used to regress field AGB against the LiDAR-derived variables to determine the best estimation model for each year. The best regression models were used to estimate AGB of 2012 and 2017. The AGB change rates (Mg/ha/year) were calculated based on the LiDAR esitmated AGB 2012 and AGB 2017. Refer Loh et al. (2022) for detailed descriptions.\nLoh,H.Y, James,D., Ioki,K., Wong,W.V.C., Tsuyuki,S., Phua,M.-H. (2022) Estimating Aboveground Biomass Changes in a Human-Modified Tropical Montane Forest of Borneo Using Multi-Temporal Airborne LiDAR Data. Remote Sensing Applications: Society and Environment, Vol.28, November 2022, 100821; doi.org/10.1016/j.rsase.2022.100821"
    ],
